.TH APRS 1
.SH NAME
aprs \- APRS over AX.25
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aprs setup
.I call
.RI [ path ]
.br
.B aprs pos
.I lat lon sym
.RI [ text ]
.br
.B aprs status
.I text
.br
.B aprs tlm
.I seq v1 v2 v3
.br
.B aprs msg
.I tocall text
.br
.B aprs hear
.RI [ n ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
APRS is not a protocol under AX.25, it is a format carried in one: an
unnumbered information frame, control
.BR 0x03 ,
PID
.BR 0xf0 ,
to a destination that is not a station, with a text information field
whose first character says what the rest is.
.I Ax25 (3)
already sends that frame, carries a digipeater path and repeats one, so
what is left is the text, and that is all this is.
.PP
.B Setup
attaches
.B /dev/radio0/data
to
.B #a
and sets this station's callsign, and turns identification on at ten
minutes, which is what a station transmitting on an amateur band is
required to do.
.PP
.B Pos
sends a position report with no timestamp:
.B !
then eight characters of latitude, the symbol table, nine of longitude,
the symbol code, and free text.
.B "5130.10N"
is 51 degrees 30.10 minutes north and
.B "00005.20W"
is 0 degrees 5.20 minutes west.  Coordinates are given in that form
rather than in degrees because that is the form; converting from
decimal degrees is arithmetic somebody else can do, and getting the
field widths wrong is the mistake that makes a beacon unreadable.
.B S
on the
.B /
table is the satellite symbol.
.PP
.B Status
sends
.BR > text .
.B Tlm
sends a sequence number and five analogue values as
.BR T#ddd,ddd,... ,
clamped to 0 through 255 and wrapping at 1000, which is the format and
not a choice.
.B Msg
sends
.BI : addressee : text
with the addressee padded to exactly nine characters, which is also not
negotiable: a message whose addressee field is short is addressed to
somebody else.
.PP
.B Hear
announces, turns
.I ax25 (3)'s
.B monitor
on and decodes what arrives, printing the frame in the form every APRS
tool reads,
.IB source > destination : information\fR,
and then what the first character says it is.
.PP
The destination is
.BR APZINF .
.B APZ
is the prefix reserved for software with no allocated tocall and the
rest is what the software calls itself.
.SH EXAMPLE
On the satellite:
.PP
.EX
	aprs setup GB1SAT-3
	aprs pos 5130.10N 00005.20W S ok
	aprs status in-orbit
	aprs tlm 7 214 33 98
.EE
.PP
and on the ground,
.BR "aprs setup W1AW-2" ,
.BR "aprs hear 3" :
.PP
.EX
	APRS GB1SAT-3>APZINF:!5130.10N/00005.20WSok
	APRS-KIND position
	APRS GB1SAT-3>APZINF:>in-orbit
	APRS-KIND status
	APRS GB1SAT-3>APZINF:T#007,214,033,098,000,000,00000000
	APRS-KIND telemetry
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/aprs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.I ax25 (3),
.I ax25raw (1),
.I radio (3)
.SH BUGS
The source callsign is read from
.BI #a/ n /remote
after the frame, not handed over with it, so on a channel with more
than one station talking it can be the wrong one.
.I Radio (3)
makes exactly this argument about
.B pkt
and solves it by putting the ancillary data in the same queue entry;
.I ax25 (3)
has no equivalent yet.
.PP
This will not be heard by the APRS network.  What goes on the air here
is an AX.25 frame without HDLC: no flags, no bit stuffing, no NRZI and
no frame check sequence, because the LoRa PHY delimits the packet and
checks it.  A TNC expects HDLC \- 1200 baud AFSK, or 9600 baud with
G3RUH's scrambler \- and will hear nothing.  Two stations running this
hear each other perfectly and nobody else.
.PP
Only the formats above are generated, and
.B hear
names the kind of a frame rather than parsing it.  Position reports
with timestamps, compressed positions, objects, items and queries are
recognised and not decoded.
